#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# csc

#% text
# `csc(x)` gives the cosecant 1/sin(x).

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `csc(x)` stays unevaluated for symbolic `x`; where `sin(x)` has an exact value, `csc(x)` is its reciprocal.

#% item
# Machine numbers, balls and complex numbers give numerical values.

#% item
# `csc` is listable.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
csc(pi/6)
#% output
#= 2

#% cell
csc(1.0)
#% output
#= 1.1883951057781212

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Symbolic arguments and symmetry:

#% cell
csc(-x)
#% output
#= -csc(x)

#% text
# 30 digits:

#% cell
n(csc(1), 30)
#% output
#= 1.1883951057781212162615994523745510

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `sin`, `sin`, `cos`, `tan`
